{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-sects", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-03-01-nicodemus-ruler", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: 'the Jews had for a great while had three sects of philosophy... the third sort of opinions was that of those called Pharisees' - the party John 3:1 places Nicodemus in existed in this period. It attests the party, not Nicodemus's membership."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-03-01-nicodemus-ruler", "grade": "C", "note": "Ananus 'assembled the sanhedrim of judges' in Jerusalem in 62 CE - the ruling council that 'a ruler of the Jews' plausibly denotes. It attests the institution, not Nicodemus's membership."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-03-23-aenon-baptism", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: John 'commanded the Jews to exercise virtue... and so to come to baptism' and 'others also came together in crowds about him' - John's baptizing activity and the crowds it drew, independent of the Gospels and in the same period and region. It does not name Aenon near Salim. Regraded B to C by architect review: a grade covers the whole claim, and Josephus attests John baptizing crowds but does not name Aenon or Salim."}
{"from": "text:eusebius-onomasticon-aenon", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-03-23-aenon-baptism", "grade": "D", "note": "Eusebius (Onomasticon 190): Aenon 'near Saleim, where John baptized according to the Gospel of John... pointed out even now eight miles south of Scythopolis near Salim'. A 4th-century site tradition reported by an author who knows John's Gospel (reception), and the identification is disputed (Tell Shalem/Salumias vs the Wadi Far'a springs vs the Madaba map's second Aenon near Jericho)."}
{"from": "text:eusebius-onomasticon-aenon", "type": "attests", "to": "place:aenon-near-salim", "grade": "D", "note": "Eusebius names Aenon near Salim as shown 'even now eight miles south of Scythopolis' - a 4th-century toponym tradition, but the identification with any excavated site is disputed (Salumias/Tell Shalem, Wadi Far'a, Madaba map's Aenon east of the Jordan)."}
{"from": "text:talmud-taanit-19b-nakdimon", "type": "attests", "to": "person:nicodemus", "grade": "D", "note": "Taanit 19b-20a knows Nakdimon ben Guryon, 'one of the wealthy citizens of Jerusalem' of the First Jewish-Roman War generation; the Jewish Encyclopedia (Ochser and Kohler) identified him with Nicodemus, while others (Carson; Bauckham) reject the identification on chronology. Disputed identification, and the Talmud was redacted c. 500 CE, long after the 30s."}
{"from": "observation:salim-springs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-03-23-aenon-baptism", "grade": "C", "note": "Gibson (2004) counted thirteen springs in a 4x4 km area south of Beth She'an near Tell Shalem - plentiful water in the candidate region ('because there was much water there'); the identification of Aenon itself is disputed, so this is context, not proof of the site."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-mikvaot-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-03-25-purification-dispute", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Mikvaot 1:1 grades immersion pools by validity - Jewish purification by immersion in ritual baths was an established, regulated custom. The Mishnah was redacted c. 200 CE and may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly; it does not attest this specific dispute."}
{"from": "claim:jn-03-23-aenon-baptism", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-03-06-baptized-in-jordan", "note": "John 3:23 locates John's continued baptizing at Aenon near Salim 'because there was much water there'; Matthew 3:6 has the crowds baptized in the Jordan river itself."}
{"from": "claim:jn-03-24-john-not-imprisoned", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-04-12-john-arrested", "note": "John 3:24 keeps John free and baptizing while Jesus works in Judea; Matthew 4:12 dates John's arrest before Jesus's Galilean ministry begins. A chronology difference between John and the Synoptics."}
{"from": "claim:jn-03-22-jesus-judea-baptizing", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-03-13-jesus-baptized", "note": "John 3:22 has Jesus himself baptizing in Judea (John 4:2 clarifies his disciples did the actual baptizing); Matthew 3:13 tells of Jesus being baptized by John. Different events; no Synoptic Gospel reports Jesus baptizing anyone."}
{"from": "claim:jn-03-14-moses-serpent", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:numbers-21-9", "note": "Numbers 21:9: 'Moses made a serpent of brass, and set it on the pole' - the biblical cross-reference behind Jesus's allusion; not independent evidence."}
{"from": "text:2-kings-18-4-nehushtan", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-03-14-moses-serpent", "note": "2 Kings 18:4 records the bronze serpent (Nehushtan) that Moses made, which stood until Hezekiah broke it - the same wilderness object John 3:14 recalls; a biblical cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "passage:jn-03-23", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-03-05", "note": "The same ongoing event: crowds from Judea and the region around the Jordan go out to John (Matthew); John 3:23 has people coming to him at Aenon near Salim."}
{"from": "passage:jn-03-23", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-03-06", "note": "The same event: John baptizing those who come to him; Matthew locates it in the Jordan, John 3:23 at Aenon near Salim."}
{"from": "passage:jn-03-23", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:lk-03-03", "note": "The same event: John's baptizing activity in the region; Luke places it in all the region around the Jordan, John 3:23 at Aenon near Salim."}
